{"step_index":60,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T20:13:30Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-22T01:43:30+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-22T01:43:42+05:30\nThe search for \"how to rebrand rename open source LLM model completely change name config tokenizer 2025 2026\" returned the following summary:\nRebranding or renaming an open-source Large Language Model (LLM) involves more than just changing a display name; it requires updating configuration files, potentially retraining or reconfiguring the tokenizer, and managing metadata to ensure compatibility with inference engines.\n\n### 1. Renaming and Configuration\nTo \"rebrand\" a model in a technical sense, you must modify its configuration and metadata so that it is recognized by software (like Hugging Face `transformers` or `vLLM`) under your new name.\n\n*   **Update `config.json`:** Every Hugging Face model contains a `config.json` file. This file dictates how the model architecture is loaded. You can change the `architectures` or custom model names here, but be careful: if you rename the class or architecture types, you may need to update the corresponding `modeling.py` files to match.\n*   **Update Model Metadata:** When saving your model using `model.save_pretrained(\"new-model-name\")`, the library automatically generates a new directory structure. Ensure you update any hardcoded strings in your `config.json` (such as `name_or_path`) to reflect your new brand identity.\n*   **Serving Configurations:** If you are using deployment tools like `vLLM` or `Ollama`, you must update the model name in your `settings.yaml`, `docker-compose.yml`, or environment variables. Some tools use the directory name as the identifier, while others look at the internal `model_name` tag.\n\n### 2. Tokenizer Rebranding & Modification\nThe tokenizer is a critical component that maps text to the integers the model understands. If you are \"completely changing\" the model, you may need to address the tokenizer as well.\n\n*   **Training a New Tokenizer:** If your new brand identity or domain requires a different vocabulary (e.g., medical or code-specific), you can train a new tokenizer using the `transformers` library:\n    ```python\n    from tokenizers import ByteLevelBPETokenizer\n    tokenizer = ByteLevelBPETokenizer()\n    tokenizer.train(files=[\"your_data.txt\"], vocab_size=32000, min_frequency=2)\n    tokenizer.save_model(\"new-tokenizer-directory\")\n    ```\n*   **Replacing the Tokenizer:** If you change the tokenizer, **you must usually retrain or fine-tune the model's embedding layers**. The model expects the vocabulary to be exactly the same as what it was trained on; changing the tokenizer without adjusting the embedding matrix will lead to nonsensical output.\n*   **Tokenizer.json:** The `tokenizer.json` file is the source of truth for modern Hugging Face tokenizers. You can manually inspect or modify this to remap tokens, though this is an advanced task that can easily break model performance if not done correctly.\n\n### 3. Best Practices for Rebranding\n*   **Maintain Compatibility:** Even if you rename the model, keep the original architecture and `model_type` in the config if you want it to remain compatible with existing ecosystem tools (e.g., if it is a Llama-3-based model, keep `model_type: \"llama\"` so that `vLLM` and other engines can still run it without custom code).\n*   **Transparency:** If your model is a derivative of another (e.g., a fine-tuned version of Llama or Mistral), always disclose this in the `README.md` and licensing documentation to adhere to the original open-source license.\n*   **Version Control:** Store your `config.json`, `tokenizer.json`, and training scripts in a repository. Use a `context.md` or similar file to document the branding changes and the rationale behind specific configuration tweaks so that future maintainers can reproduce your work.\n*   **Testing:** After renaming and reconfiguring, run a \"perplexity\" check or standard benchmarks. Even simple changes to the config or tokenizer can introduce subtle bugs that degrade model performance."}
